      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The subtle art of memory leaks in C++. It's like trying to catch a mischievous goblin in a well-hidden lair. \n\nOne common way memory leaks can hide is through the use of smart pointers, which are designed to automatically manage memory for you. However, if not used correctly, they can lead to memory leaks. For example, if you have a `std::unique_ptr` that is not properly reset or released, it can lead to a memory leak.\n\nAnother way memory leaks can hide is through the use of `std::vector` and other dynamic arrays. These containers can lead to memory fragmentation and slow leaks over time, especially if they are not properly cleared or resized.\n\nIn your case, the `history` vector is a good example of this. Even if you fix the vector, you may still be experiencing memory leaks due to other parts of your code.\n\nHere's an example of how a memory leak can hide in a `std::vector`:\n\n```cpp\nvoid TelemetryManager::processBuffer(const uint8_t* data, size_t len) {\n    // ...\n    history.push_back(len);\n    // ...\n}\n\nvoid TelemetryManager::clearHistory() {\n    history.clear();\n    // ...\n}",
